  "type": "say",
  "text": "Thank you both. Let me make sure I have this straight before I make any decision. Jon, you're saying the reclaimed material can be suitable but only with a specific blend ratio and mandatory first-article testing, and the lab documentation still needs to be attached to the production traveler. Priya, you're telling me the revised SDS classifies the additive residue as regulated process waste, which means segregated labeled storage and a hazardous-waste manifest for any discarded pellets or purge material. That has direct implications for the environmental-compliance sign-off I need. Before I can authorize anything, I need to see two things happen: first, Jon, I need you to formally put on the record the exact blend ratio and the number of first housings required for first-article testing. Second, Priya, I need your formal environmental-compliance sign-off or denial — can you give me that now, or do you need additional time to complete your review? I also need confirmation from both of you that the documentation, storage, and waste-control records will be in place before the run begins. Let's get those specifics locked down."
}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Mara, I cannot give a formal environmental-compliance sign-off at this time. My review of the revised SDS is complete, and I've already placed my findings on the record — the additive residue is classified as regulated process waste, triggering labeled segregated storage and hazardous-waste manifest requirements for any discarded pellets or purge material. However, before I can sign off, the following must happen: first, the revised SDS must be entered in the plant's chemical register, which has not yet occurred. Second, the labeled segregated storage for the reclaimed lot must be verified and recorded — right now the lot is sitting in the materials cage without that designation. Third, the hazardous-waste manifest procedures for discarded pellets and purge material from this run must be established and documented before production begins. I also need confirmation that the waste-control and storage records Mara referenced will reflect these hazardous-waste designations specifically, not just standard material-approval records. If those conditions are met and documented, I can provide a formal sign-off. If they cannot be in place before Thursday, I would have to deny the sign-off. I need to ask the group: is there sufficient time and capability to get the chemical register updated, segregated storage established, and manifest procedures in place before Thursday's run?"}
```

```json
{
  "type": "say",
  "text": "Thank you both for getting those specifics on the record. Here's where we stand. Jon has formally specified a 15% reclaimed-to-85% virgin blend ratio, with the first 50 molded housings requiring FTIR identity testing as first-article testing, and the lab documentation still needs to be attached to the production traveler. Priya has laid out three conditions that must be met and documented before she can provide environmental-compliance sign-off: the revised SDS entered in the chemical register, labeled segregated storage verified and recorded for the reclaimed lot, and hazardous-waste manifest procedures established and documented for discarded pellets and purge material. She has also confirmed that if those conditions cannot be in place before Thursday, she would deny the sign-off. Per plant policy, I cannot authorize reclaimed material for this regulated-product run without both quality and environmental-compliance sign-off. So the decision before us is clear: can these three environmental conditions and Jon's documentation gap all be closed before Thursday? I need to hear from the group on feasibility and timing before I make my final authorization decision. If the conditions cannot be met, I will not authorize the reclaimed material, and we will need to address the virgin polymer shortfall another way."
}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Mara, on the quality side, I can commit to closing my documentation gap before Thursday. I will attach the lab review results — including the 15%-to-85% blend ratio specification and the FTIR first-article testing requirement for the first 50 molded housings — to the production traveler before the run begins. That is fully within my control and I can have it done promptly. However, I want to be clear on scope: my quality sign-off covers the material suitability, blend ratio, and first-article testing requirements I've already placed on the record. The environmental-compliance conditions Priya outlined — the chemical register update, segregated labeled storage, and hazardous-waste manifest procedures — are outside my area and I cannot speak to their feasibility or timing. I'll defer to Priya and others on whether those can realistically be established and documented before Thursday. From my standpoint, if the environmental sign-off comes through and my documentation gap is closed, the quality conditions for the run would be met."}
```

